RULE NUMBER
002

TITLE
Forge: Add validation rules to spec.proto (buf.validate + CEL)

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer’s local workspace in Cursor. This rule only adds validations to an existing `spec.proto`. Do not change the schema.

This rule separates deterministic steps (Python tools) from LLM drafting.

SCOPE
- Target: `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/v1alpha1/spec.proto`
- Only add validations and brief comments. No field/message renames/additions/deletions.
- Do not manually create folders; writer tool handles directories.
- Do not edit other files.

TOOLS
- Reader: `_rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/spec_proto_reader.py`
- Writer+Build: `_rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/spec_proto_write_and_build.py`
- Validation standard: the "VALIDATION MESSAGE QUALITY" section in this rule (below) is the
  authoritative standard. This rule is the canonical home for the Validation Message Philosophy.
- Reference kinds (live ground truth): study the validated `spec.proto` of
  `catalog/aws/awsvpc/v1alpha1/spec.proto` and `catalog/aws/awsalb/v1alpha1/spec.proto` for
  buf.validate field options, message-level CEL, and message wording.

SEQUENTIAL STEPS
1) Read: `python3 _rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/spec_proto_reader.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder>`
2) Draft: Following the VALIDATION MESSAGE QUALITY section below and the reference kind
   `spec.proto` files, add validations to the `spec.proto` content (import buf/validate, field
   options, message-level CEL). Do not change schema.
3) Write+Build: `cat <<'PROTO' | python3 _rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/spec_proto_write_and_build.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --stdin | cat`
   - If `build_succeeded` is false, refine validations and retry (≤3).

VALIDATION MESSAGE QUALITY (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

Every CEL `message` string you write will be read by a human configuring this kind. You are not a compiler emitting errors -- you are a knowledgeable colleague reviewing their configuration and guiding them to success.

This is the Validation Message Philosophy. It is mandatory, not optional. The checklist below
is the full standard -- there is no separate guide to read; treat these rules as authoritative
and confirm your wording against the reference kind `spec.proto` files named in TOOLS.

Quick checklist for every `message:` string:
- Is it a complete sentence a non-expert can act on? (not "required" or "invalid")
- Does it explain the constraint in plain English? (not raw regex)
- For regex patterns: is the regex in a code comment with the human description in the message?
- For conditional rules: does it explain WHEN and WHY?
- Would you be proud of this message if a developer saw it in their first hour using this kind?

Reject and rewrite any message that is terse, generic, or exposes implementation details.

CEL ON `StringValueOrRef` FIELDS (REFERENCE-SAFE PRESENCE CHECKS)

A `StringValueOrRef` field can arrive as a literal (`value`) OR a reference
(`value_from`). A CEL presence check written as `this.field.value != ''` silently
REJECTS valid manifests that use `valueFrom` — the reference branch has no `value`,
so the expression is false even though the field is set. This shipped as a real
defect (GcpGlobalAddress `internal_requires_network` rejected VPC-ref manifests
before it was caught and fixed).

- Correct presence check: `has(this.field) && (has(this.field.value) || has(this.field.value_from))`
- Worked examples: `catalog/gcp/gcpcloudsql/v1alpha1/spec.proto` (`connectivity_required`),
  `catalog/gcp/gcpaddress/v1alpha1/spec.proto` (`peering_purpose_requires_network`).
- `this.field.value != ''` is only safe when the rule really is about the LITERAL
  branch specifically (rare; document why in a comment if so).

GCP REGION/ZONE PATTERNS MUST ACCEPT MULTI-DIGIT REGIONS

GCP region numbers are not single digits: `europe-west12`, `me-central2`, and
their zones (`europe-west12-a`) are real locations. A pattern written as
`^[a-z]+-[a-z]+[0-9]$` (or `...[0-9]-[a-z]$` for zones) silently rejects them.
This shipped as a real defect across six GCP kinds before a review caught it.

- Region: `^[a-z]+-[a-z]+[0-9]+$`
- Zone: `^[a-z]+-[a-z]+[0-9]+-[a-z]$`
- Region-or-zone (one field spanning both): `^[a-z]+-[a-z]+[0-9]+(-[a-z])?$`
- Include a multi-digit case (e.g. `europe-west12`) in the spec test whenever a
  location pattern is introduced.

EXCLUSION RULES AGAINST A DEFAULTED OPTIONAL MUST TOLERATE THE DEFAULT
A mutual-exclusion CEL that forbids setting field B alongside arm A —
written as `!this.a || !has(this.b)` — silently makes arm A UNEXPRESSIBLE
when B is an `optional` field carrying a spec default: the platform's
defaulting middleware stamps the default onto every manifest before
validation, so `has(this.b)` is true for manifests that never declared B.
Spec tests cannot catch it (they validate the raw message, not the
defaulted one); the failure surfaces only at manifest validation. Tolerate
exactly the default value — `!this.a || (!has(this.b) || this.b ==
'<default>') …` — with a comment naming the middleware as the reason, have
the module ignore the defaulted value when arm A is active, and add BOTH
spec-test cases: arm A with the default value accepted, arm A with a
non-default value rejected.

NEGATED all() OVER AN OPTIONAL REPEATED FIELD IS VACUOUSLY VIOLATED
CEL's `all()` over an EMPTY list is true, so an exclusivity rule written as
`!this.all(x, <bad-shape>)` on a repeated field FIRES on every manifest that
omits the list entirely — the minimal valid manifest fails validation, and
nothing catches it offline unless a spec test covers the omitted-list case.
When mirroring an upstream CRD's `!self.all(...)`-shaped rule (upstream CEL
only runs when the field exists; a proto repeated field is always "present"),
guard it with `this.size() == 0 || …` — UNLESS the list carries
`min_items >= 1`, where the guard is dead code and should be omitted. Always
include an omits-the-optional-list accept case in the spec tests for any
message-level or repeated-field rule.

RequiredWith MIRRORS ARGUMENT PRESENCE, NOT VALUE PRESENCE
When mirroring a provider schema's `RequiredWith` pairing as a CEL, remember it
requires the paired ARGUMENT to be present in configuration -- an EMPTY value can
be legal (e.g. a certificate blob paired with a password argument, where
passwordless bundles exist and the provider's own acceptance fixtures include
one). A both-or-neither CEL over string values (`(a == '') == (b == '')`) turns
such a pairing into an INVENTED rule that rejects legal empty-value shapes.
Before pairing values, check the provider's acceptance-test fixtures for
empty-value usage; when empty is legal, validate only the direction that is a
genuine contradiction (the dependent value without its anchor) and have both
modules send the possibly-empty paired argument whenever the anchor is set.

SUCCESS
- Build succeeds with validations (`build_succeeded: true`).
- No schema changes.
- Validations present for core fields and important constraints.
- Every CEL `message` is a clear, actionable, user-facing string (see quality checklist above).
- Every secret-bearing field is annotated `(sensitive) = true`; every look-alike non-secret
  carries a `sensitive_exempt_reason`; no field carries both.
- `planton secret-coverage --check` passes (no new gap, no stale baseline entry, no
  self-contradictory annotation).

OUTPUT
- Show created/updated path and key JSON fields from tools.
- On success: confirm file path. On error: brief fix and rerun (≤3).
DEFAULT FIELD OPTIONS (SEPARATE FROM VALIDATION)
- Default values use `(dev.planton.shared.options.default)` NOT buf.validate
- Import: `import "shared/options/options.proto";`
- Fields with defaults MUST be marked as `optional`
- Example combining both: `optional string tag = 1 [(dev.planton.shared.options.default) = "2.331.0", (buf.validate.field).string.min_len = 1];`
- The `DEFAULT_REQUIRES_OPTIONAL` linter enforces this pattern at build time
- See the reference kind `spec.proto` files named in TOOLS for worked examples

VERIFY DEFAULTS DURING VALIDATION PASS
- When adding validations, also verify that all fields identified in rule 001 as having
  defaults actually carry the `optional` keyword and `(dev.planton.shared.options.default)`.
- If you find a field whose comment says "defaults to X" but lacks the formal field option,
  add both `optional` and the default option now (this counts as adding options, not schema change).
- Defaults and validations are complementary, not alternatives:
  - A field can have BOTH a default AND a validation (e.g., `min_len = 1` + default = "standard")
  - The default ensures the field is populated; the validation ensures the value is acceptable
- Example: `optional string content_type = 4 [(dev.planton.shared.options.default) = "application/octet-stream"];`

SENSITIVE FIELD OPTIONS (SEPARATE FROM VALIDATION)
- Secret-by-default is a field OPTION, not a buf.validate rule. Annotate every
  secret-bearing field identified in rule 001 so the platform forces it to be a
  managed-secret reference (never plaintext).
- Import: `import "shared/options/options.proto";` (same import as defaults).
- Annotate a secret: `[(dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive) = true]`. Per the option's
  doc comment, this "applies to string fields and to the literal value of
  `StringValueOrRef` fields."
  - Example (plain string): `string master_password = 10 [(buf.validate.field).required = true, (dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive) = true];`
- Exempt a look-alike that is NOT a secret with a justification string:
  `[(dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive_exempt_reason) = "Access-key id (a public identifier), not a secret value."]`.
  - Set `sensitive` OR `sensitive_exempt_reason` on a field, NEVER both -- the coverage
    gate rejects the contradiction.
- FK composition: a `StringValueOrRef` field may carry BOTH
  `(dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive) = true` AND its foreign-key annotation
  `(dev.planton.shared.foreignkey.v1.default_kind) = ...`. They compose -- annotate
  `sensitive` on the field as usual; the reference branch is unaffected (downstream the
  field offers a managed-secret reference OR a cross-resource reference, never plaintext).
- `sensitive` and `default`/validations are complementary: a field may carry a default,
  a buf.validate rule, AND `sensitive` together.
- Authority for the option semantics: the `sensitive` / `sensitive_exempt_reason` field
  comments in `shared/options/options.proto`.

VERIFY SENSITIVE COVERAGE (RUN THE GATE)
- After annotating, run the developer gate: `planton secret-coverage --check`. It fails on:
  - a NEW gap (a field that looks sensitive by name but is neither annotated nor exempted),
  - a STALE baseline entry,
  - a self-contradictory annotation, or
  - a POINTLESS exemption (`sensitive_exempt_reason` on a field whose name does not trip
    the heuristic at all -- e.g. an `*_id` suffix reads as an identifier even when the
    name contains "secret", as in `key_vault_secret_id`).
  The error text states the exact fix ("annotate it `sensitive`, or exempt it with
  `sensitive_exempt_reason`").
- Consequence of the pointless-exemption class: do NOT exempt speculatively while
  authoring. Annotate the true secrets in the schema, then RUN the gate and exempt only
  the exact fields it reports -- the gate's heuristic, not your reading of the field
  name, decides what needs an exemption.
- CI runs the same gate as `go test ./pkg/secretcoverage/...`
  (`.github/workflows/lint.secret-coverage.yaml`) -- it must be green.
- A genuine, deliberate deferral (rare) is recorded as an accepted gap with
  `planton secret-coverage --write-baseline`; do NOT use the baseline to silence a field
  that should simply be annotated.
